*Create: POST /article

  Create builds a new entity from the validated body.  It then saves the entity and responds 201.

%{snippet|file=${basedir}/src/main/resources/officefloor/rest/article.POST.yml}

  <<<BuildArticle>>> is the producer for a create.  As the first function it carries the
  <<<@Valid @RequestBody>>>.  Validation runs before anything is persisted.

%{snippet|id=tutorial|file=${basedir}/src/main/java/net/officefloor/tutorial/scratchopenapi/BuildArticle.java}

  <<<SaveArticle>>> is a pure persistence function.  It works only on the entity.
  <<<RespondWithArticleCreated>>> maps the saved entity to a DTO.  It adds the 201 status and the Location
  header.

%{snippet|id=tutorial|file=${basedir}/src/main/java/net/officefloor/tutorial/scratchopenapi/SaveArticle.java}

%{snippet|id=tutorial|file=${basedir}/src/main/java/net/officefloor/tutorial/scratchopenapi/RespondWithArticleCreated.java}


*Update: PUT /article/\{id\}

  Update is the fullest pipeline.  It shows why validation is its own function.

%{snippet|file=${project.build.directory}/site-snippets/article/id.PUT.yml}

  The order matters.  The request body can be read only once.  <<<@Valid>>> runs before its function's body.
  If <<<Load>>> ran first, an invalid body against a missing id would return 404 before the body was
  checked.  So <<<ValidateArticle>>> runs first.  It validates the body and publishes it as a variable for
  a later function.  The body is consumed exactly once.

%{snippet|id=tutorial|file=${basedir}/src/main/java/net/officefloor/tutorial/scratchopenapi/ValidateArticle.java}

  <<<LoadArticle>>> is reused unchanged from the read.  <<<ApplyArticle>>> copies the validated request
  onto the loaded entity.  It reads two variables and mutates the entity in place.

%{snippet|id=tutorial|file=${basedir}/src/main/java/net/officefloor/tutorial/scratchopenapi/ApplyArticle.java}

  <<<SaveArticle>>> and <<<RespondWithArticle>>> are reused from create and read.  The same 200 responder
  serves both reading and updating an article.


*Invalid input: escalation to 400

  Validation failures escalate the same way not-found does.  A handler for Spring's
  <<<MethodArgumentNotValidException>>> maps it to a 400.  The <<<Validate>>> and <<<Build>>> functions stay
  free of error handling.

%{snippet|file=${basedir}/src/main/resources/officefloor/escalation/org.springframework.web.bind.MethodArgumentNotValidException.yml}

%{snippet|id=tutorial|file=${basedir}/src/main/java/net/officefloor/tutorial/scratchopenapi/ValidationExceptionHandler.java}


*Delete: DELETE /article/\{id\}

  Delete loads the row, deletes it, then responds 204.  It reuses <<<LoadArticle>>> and its 404.  It has no
  body.  The responder is the shared <<<RespondWithNoContent>>>.  Every no-content endpoint uses it.

